<!--
    Kuna pushan in-function interpreters: P2 flow-follow, option pushan
    (default auto).  The script loads the x86-64 PIE
    decompiler/crates/kuna-analysis/tests/fixtures/pushan_interp_x86_64
    (source pushan_interp_x86_64.c, gcc -O0): nine functions that are their
    own bytecode interpreters, the shape Tigress Virtualize gives a function,
    each running `s = 0; for (i = 0; i < 10; i++) s += a < i ? i : a;
    return s;` with its virtual PC in a local.  sw_writable dispatches by a
    switch on an opcode byte in writable .data, sw_readonly the same from
    .rodata, th_copied by `goto **pc` through handler addresses it copies
    from writable data into a 32 KiB local with memcpy (after gcc's stack
    probe), th_inline the same with the copy an inline `rep movsq`, wv_woven by a switch through pc[sel] over two programs run
    round-robin (the second a bogus endless loop), sw_patched by a switch
    over bytecode it patches with its argument, and tg_indexed by a switch
    in Tigress's style, every variable reached through its slot's address
    pushed on the virtual stack, over a second program that writes st[1]
    under a branch and reads st[1..3] only through &st[0] plus a computed
    offset.

    Pass 1 (`option pushan off`) is the bug: sw_writable decompiles to its
    interpreter, its virtual PC pointed at sw_code (#1) and a loop around
    the switch on the fetched opcode, with no trace of the virtual program.
    Pass 2 (the default) finds each interpreter after the plain flow showed a
    computed jump: nine devirtualized headers (#2).  The fetches through the
    VPC read the file's bytes; the eight functions whose bytecode is in
    writable memory say the body assumes it is what the file holds, and
    sw_readonly does not (#3).  The virtual loop and branch come back in the
    five complete programs (#4, #5, #6); the memcpy stays a call, and the
    `rep movsq` that only the folded fetches read is left out, so no copy
    loop remains (#7, #10), the woven function follows its first program and
    says so (#8), and
    sw_patched, whose patched opcode decides a dispatch, is reported
    incomplete (#9).  tg_indexed keeps the stores to st and the branch that
    only the indexed loop reads (#11, #12): the loop's LOAD goes through
    &st[0] spilled to the virtual stack, and before the heritage followed
    such spills its guard was missing and they were removed as dead.
    tg_istore (an eighth function) writes st[0..3] through &st[0] plus a
    computed offset in a loop and returns st[1] + st[3]: before every store
    into a devirtualized body's frame was guarded, the loop's stores left the
    slots' SSA values untouched, the C returned a0 * 2 + 4 (the values stored
    before the loop) and the stores of st[1..3] were dropped (#12, #13, #14).
    tg_carried (a ninth) updates st[2] in its loop and reads the array only
    through &st[0] plus a computed offset.  The guard of that LOAD forced
    the writes it reaches only below the lowest slot a stack pointer was
    spilled to, and the interpreter's operand-stack slots, which hold
    pointers and values in turn, made every slot of st such a spill: the
    loop's update and the stores of st[1..3] lost their reader and were
    deleted, and in tg_istore the store of st[2] (#12, #15, #16).
-->
<bytechunk space="ram" offset="0x100000" readonly="true">
c3
</bytechunk>
